{
  "answer": {
    "answer_text": "Your legs most likely ache because of delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS). It's caused by over-exertion, where micro-damage to the muscle triggers an inflammatory response. It isn't lactic acid. The body clears lactic acid from the muscles within a few hours.\n\n- **Timing:** Muscle pain sets in one to three days after intense exercise.\n- **Triggers:** DOMS usually follows a new activity or a workout harder than you're used to. Downhill running is a common cause, because the muscle is working as it lengthens.\n- **Relief:** A warm bath after training can reduce DOMS. Gentle movement may also help.\n\nIt's usually harmless. If the pain is sharp, severe or persistent, get it checked out. The same goes for swelling in a leg. In the UK, you can call NHS 111 or see your GP.",
